; A restriction over an EFFECT-TRANSPARENT function: AX3051, the THIRD
; reading of "unverifiable".
;
; `docs/reference.md` (Effect Polymorphism) says a higher-order
; function's summary has two halves - the effects its own body performs
; concretely, and the parameters it CALLS - and that claims are
; validated against the concrete half. `;@axiom:pure` on `(fn (apply f
; x) (f x))` stands under that reading, and should: "pure modulo its
; function parameters" is what purity means on a higher-order function.
;
; A RESTRICTION IS NOT THAT KIND OF CLAIM. `restrict(no-io)` reads to
; its author as "calling this performs no IO", and for a body that
; calls one of its own parameters that is the CALLER's argument's
; business. Measured before this case existed: `(fn (runIt f n) { (f
; "x") n })` under `restrict(no-io)` checked `OK` with no diagnostic at
; all, and the program printed to stdout. The same silence covered
; `no-alloc`, `no-foreign`, `no-recursion` and `no-cast:deep`, and
; `no-recursion` in particular was silent over a program that really
; does recurse - `runIt -> back -> runIt`, a cycle whose middle edge is
; a parameter the graph has no edge for.
;
; The compiler KNEW: `symbols` renders `#effect-params=f` on exactly
; these rows. The marker existed and the restriction checker did not
; read it, which is the same shape as the `restrict(no-alloc)`
; constructor hole - a checked claim that could not fail.
;
; Five warnings, one per transitive restriction, and five controls:
;
;   `viaIo`        no-io          -> AX3051
;   `viaAlloc`     no-alloc       -> AX3051
;   `viaForeign`   no-foreign     -> AX3051
;   `viaRec`       no-recursion   -> AX3051
;   `viaDeep`      no-cast:deep   -> AX3051
;
;   `lexCast`      no-cast        -> silent: LEXICAL, checked whether
;                                     or not a call resolved, and this
;                                     body casts nothing
;   `lexWrap`      no-wrap        -> silent: lexical, same reason
;   `named`        no-io          -> silent: the parameter is NAMED and
;                                     not called, so it is not an
;                                     effect-transparent position and
;                                     the row does not mark it
;   `plain`        no-io          -> silent: no parameter is called,
;                                     which is every function in this
;                                     tree today (measured: 0 of 347
;                                     rows carrying a restriction also
;                                     carry `#effect-params=`)
;   `bothWays`     no-io          -> AX3049 and NOT AX3051: the body
;                                     performs IO concretely, so the
;                                     claim is REFUTED rather than
;                                     unverifiable, and saying both
;                                     about one claim would be saying
;                                     two things about one fact
(import IO)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io)
(:: viaIo (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

(fn (viaIo f n) (f n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-alloc)
(:: viaAlloc (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

(fn (viaAlloc f n) (f n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-foreign)
(:: viaForeign (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

(fn (viaForeign f n) (f n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-recursion)
(:: viaRec (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

(fn (viaRec f n) (f n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-cast:deep)
(:: viaDeep (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

(fn (viaDeep f n) (f n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-cast)
(:: lexCast (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

(fn (lexCast f n) (f n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-wrap)
(:: lexWrap (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

(fn (lexWrap f n) (f n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io)
(:: named (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

(fn (named f n) (cast Int f))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io)
(:: plain (-> Int Int))

(fn (plain n) n)

;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:restrict(no-io)
(:: bothWays (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (bothWays f n) { (println "loud") (f n) })

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  {
    (viaIo plain 1)
    (viaAlloc plain 1)
    (viaForeign plain 1)
    (viaRec plain 1)
    (viaDeep plain 1)
    (lexCast plain 1)
    (lexWrap plain 1)
    (named plain 1)
    (plain 1)
    (bothWays plain 1)
  }
)
